![](/images/articles/anchor.jpeg)

"Sticker shock" is when a customer recoils in horror from the price you just gave them.

Sticker shock happens when what a customer *expects* to pay is less than the price you tell them. That expectation is the customer's *pricing anchor*.

A rep called me last month about a pricing situation. The customer was paying $20K a year. Our price was more like $80K.

The rep was panicked about putting $80K in front of the customer, for good reason; the customer was firmly anchored on the $20K of current spend. But this customer had a $1m problem to solve.

I've written before about the[ Tyranny of ER](https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/tyranny-er-steve-bamberger/) — the trap of selling on relative cost (a little fastER, cheapER, bettER than the alternative). Pricing has its own version. When you anchor on what the customer is paying today, you are silently agreeing that today's price is the relevant anchor.

$20K is not "what this thing is worth." It's what the customer agreed to pay for an older, smaller version of it, before the problem grew. Or what they're willing to pay for a product that looks similar, but doesn't actually solve their problem.

Price is simply your fair share of the value you create. And the value is defined by the problem you solve.

When you anchor on outcomes, an $80K deal to solve a $1m problem doesn't feel like a 4x increase. It feels like a discount.

Three lessons from this:

First: in a pricing conversation, you are always anchored to something. The only question is whether you chose the anchor or your customer did. If you let "what you pay today" be the anchor, you have already negotiated against yourself.

Second: business cases live with business leaders, not with procurement. Procurement's job is to reduce price, not to address the problem. If your value conversation hasn't happened with a senior business stakeholder who owns the outcome, no number you propose will hold up under pressure. (See also: my old post on getting an x-ray of the deal.)

Third: if you can't quantify the problem, you can't defend the price. Too often, we quantify the product (features, seats, integrations) and assume the customer will do the quantification of the problem on their own. They won't. That's your job — and if you don't do it before pricing, you'll do it after, while your deal is already on the ropes.

One last note for managers. When a rep tells you "the customer is pushing back on price," consider whether you're talking to the right person, and what established the customer's anchor. Then go back with them to the value conversation, and make sure you're working with someone who has the authority to exchange investment for return.
